{"id":"neet-2026r-q052","pn":52,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","st":"Ionization enthalpy trends","q":"Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.\nAssertion A: The first ionization enthalpy of O is lower than that of N and F.\nReason R: The loss of an electron from O leads to stable half-filled p orbital.\nIn light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","o":["Both A and R are correct and R is NOT the correct explanation of A","A is correct but R is not correct.","A is not correct but R is correct","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A"],"c":3,"s":"N: 1s² 2s² 2p³. F: 1s² 2s² 2p⁵.\nFirst ionization enthalpy: N = 1402, O = 1314, F = 1681 kJ mol⁻¹. So O is lower than both N and F.\nN has a stable half-filled configuration, so its ionization enthalpy is higher than O.\nO(g) → O⁺(g) + e⁻: 2s²2p⁴ → 2s²2p³ (half-filled p subshell).\nLosing an electron gives a stable half-filled p subshell, so R explains A."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q065","pn":65,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","st":"Valence orbitals and covalency","q":"The correct statement is","o":["Beryllium has three valence orbitals.","Magnesium has a maximum covalency of four.","Aluminium has five valence orbitals.","Boron has a maximum covalency of four."],"c":3,"s":"Aluminium has nine valence orbitals.\nBeryllium has four valence orbitals.\nMagnesium has a maximum covalency of six.\nBoron has a maximum covalency of four, as in BH₄⁻.","sfig":{"w":200,"h":130,"alt":"Structure of BH₄⁻: B⁻ in the centre bonded to one H above, one H below and two H at the lower left and lower right, inside brackets.","d":[["p","M58 10Q40 10 40 30L40 100Q40 120 58 120",""],["p","M142 10Q160 10 160 30L160 100Q160 120 142 120",""],["t",100,32,"H","m"],["l",100,37,100,53,""],["t",100,70,"B⁻","m"],["l",100,76,100,92,""],["t",100,108,"H","m"],["l",92,72,74,92,""],["t",68,106,"H","m"],["l",108,72,126,92,""],["t",132,106,"H","m"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2026-q079","pn":79,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","st":"Periodic trends and nomenclature","q":"Identify the incorrect statement from the following :","o":["The IUPAC name of the element with atomic number 107 is Unnilseptium.","The largest and the smallest species among Mg, Mg²⁺, Al and Al³⁺ are Al and Mg²⁺ respectively.","The similarity in behaviour of Li with Mg is referred to as 'diagonal relationship'","The oxidation state and covalency of Al in [AlCl(H₂O)₅]²⁺ are 3 and 6, respectively."],"c":1,"s":"Among Mg, Mg²⁺, Al and Al³⁺ the largest species is Mg and the smallest is Al³⁺.\nStatement 2 says Al and Mg²⁺, so it is incorrect.\nThe element with atomic number 107 is Unnilseptium.\nLi and Mg are diagonally related and have similar properties.\nIn [AlCl(H₂O)₅]²⁺ the oxidation state of Al is +3 and its covalency is 6."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q080","pn":80,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","st":"Metallic character trend","q":"The correct order of increasing metallic character of Na, Be, P, Mg and Si is","o":["P < Si < Be < Mg < Na","Be < Si < P < Mg < Na","P < Si < Na < Mg < Be","P < Mg < Be < Si < Na"],"c":0,"s":"Moving left to right in a period, metallic character decreases.\nMoving top to bottom in a group, metallic character increases.\nElectronegativity ∝ 1/(metallic character).\nPauling electronegativity: Na 0.9, Mg 1.2, Be 1.5, Si 1.8, P 2.1.\nSo the order of increasing metallic character is P < Si < Be < Mg < Na."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q085","pn":85,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","st":"Electron gain enthalpy trend","q":"Among the following options, the correct trend in the electron gain enthalpy is","o":["Br > Cl > F > I","Cl > F > Br > I","I > Br > Cl > F","F > Cl > Br > I"],"c":1,"s":"Electron gain enthalpies of group 17 (kJ/mol): F = –328, Cl = –349, Br = –325, I = –295.\nSo the correct order of electron gain enthalpy is Cl > F > Br > I."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q047","pn":47,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","st":"Periodic trends, isoelectronic species","q":"Which of the following statements are true?\nA. Unlike Ga that has a very high melting point, Cs has a very low melting point.\nB. On Pauling scale, the electronegativity values of N and Cl are not the same.\nC. Ar, K⁺, Cl⁻, Ca²⁺, and S²⁻ are all isoelectronic species.\nD. The correct order of the first ionization enthalpies of Na, Mg, Al, and Si is Si >Al > Mg > Na.\nE. The atomic radius of Cs is greater than that of Li and Rb.\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["A, B, and E only","C and E only","C and D only","A, C, and E only"],"c":1,"s":"A is false: both Ga and Cs have low melting points. Ga is 303 K and Cs is 302 K.\nB is false: on the Pauling scale, N and Cl have the same electronegativity (3.0).\nC is true: Ar, K⁺, Cl⁻, Ca²⁺ and S²⁻ all have 18 electrons, so they are isoelectronic.\nD is false: the correct order of first ionisation enthalpy is Si > Mg > Al > Na.\nFirst ionisation enthalpy of Mg is higher than Al because the penetration of a 3s electron to the nucleus is more than that of a 2p-electron (as printed; it should say 3p).\nE is true: atomic radii generally increase down the group. Li is 152 pm, Rb is 244 pm, Cs is 262 pm.\nNote: Publisher solution says 2p-electron where 3p is meant (typo); answer unchanged."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q053","pn":53,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","st":"s, p, d, f block elements","q":"Which among the following electronic configurations belong to main group elements?\nA. [Ne]3s¹\nB. [Ar]3d³4s²\nC. [Kr]4d¹⁰5s²5p⁵\nD. [Ar]3d¹⁰4s¹\nE. [Rn]5f⁰6d²7s²\nChoose the correct answer from the option given below :","o":["B and E only","A and C only","D and E only","A, C and D only"],"c":1,"s":"(A) [Ne]3s¹ is Na (s-block).\n(B) [Ar]3d³4s² is V (d-block).\n(C) [Kr]4d¹⁰5s²5p⁵ is I (p-block).\n(D) [Ar]3d¹⁰4s¹ is Cu (d-block).\n(E) [Rn]5f⁰6d²7s² is Th (f-block).\nMain group elements: A and C only."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q062","pn":62,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","st":"Electronegativity trend","q":"Arrange the following elements in increasing order of electronegativity :\nN, O, F, C, Si\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below :","o":["O < F < N < C < Si","F < O < N < C < Si","Si < C < N < O < F","Si < C < O < N < F"],"c":2,"s":"Electronegativity increases left to right across a period.\nIt decreases down a group.\nSo the order is Si < C < N < O < F."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q082","pn":82,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","st":"Ionization enthalpy trend","q":"Arrange the following elements in increasing order of first ionization enthalpy :\nLi, Be, B, C, N\nChoose the correct answer from options given below:","o":["Li < Be < C < B < N","Li < Be < N < B < C","Li < Be < B < C < N","Li < B < Be < C < N"],"c":3,"s":"Increasing order of first ionization enthalpy is Li < B < Be < C < N.\nValues (ΔiH in kJ mol⁻¹): Li 520, Be 899, B 801, C 1086, N 1402.","sfig":{"w":260,"h":170,"alt":"Table of first ionization enthalpy in kJ per mol: Li 520, Be 899, B 801, C 1086, N 1402.","d":[["r",10,10,220,130],["l",80,10,80,140],["l",10,40,230,40],["l",10,60,230,60],["l",10,80,230,80],["l",10,100,230,100],["l",10,120,230,120],["t",45,29,"Element","m"],["t",155,23,"First ionization enthalpy","m"],["t",155,36,"(ΔᵢH/kJ mol⁻¹)","m"],["t",45,54,"Li","m"],["t",155,54,"520","m"],["t",45,74,"Be","m"],["t",155,74,"899","m"],["t",45,94,"B","m"],["t",155,94,"801","m"],["t",45,114,"C","m"],["t",155,114,"1086","m"],["t",45,134,"N","m"],["t",155,134,"1402","m"]]}},
{"id":"neet-2023-q062","pn":62,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","st":"Ionic radius","q":"The element expected to form largest ion to achieve the nearest noble gas configuration is-","o":["Na","O","F","N"],"c":3,"s":"Na⁺, O²⁻, F⁻ and N³⁻ are all isoelectronic with neon.\nIn isoelectronic species the size grows as the negative charge grows.\nN forms N³⁻, which has the highest charge, so it is the largest ion."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q056","pn":56,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","st":"Ionization enthalpy trend","q":"The correct order of first ionization enthalpy for the given four elements is :","o":["C < F < N < O","C < N < F < O","C < N < O < F","C < O < N < F"],"c":3,"s":"C is 1s² 2s² 2p², N is 1s² 2s² 2p³, O is 1s² 2s² 2p⁴, F is 1s² 2s² 2p⁵.\nIonization enthalpy generally rises across the period.\nN has a half-filled 2p subshell, which is extra stable, so N is higher than O.\nOrder: C < O < N < F."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q061","pn":61,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","st":"IUPAC names of elements","q":"The IUPAC name of an element with atomic number 119 is","o":["unnilennium","unununnium","ununoctium","ununennium"],"c":3,"s":"The digits 1, 1, 9 are written as un, un, enn, and the ending ium is added.\nThe name is ununennium."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q083","pn":83,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Classification of Elements and Periodicity in Properties","st":"Ionisation enthalpy trends","q":"If first ionization enthalpies of elements X and Y are 419 kJ mol⁻¹ and 590 kJ mol⁻¹, respectively and second ionization enthalpies of X and Y are 3069 kJ mol⁻¹ and 1145 kJ mol⁻¹, respectively.\nThen correct statement is :","o":["Both X and Y are alkaline earth metals.","X is an alkali metal and Y is an alkaline earth metal.","X is an alkaline earth metal and Y is an alkali metal.","Both X and Y are alkali metals."],"c":1,"s":"X has a low first ionisation enthalpy and a very high second one.\nAfter losing one electron it gets a noble gas configuration, so removing a second electron is very hard. So X is an alkali metal.\nY has a higher first value because of the stable ns² configuration, but its second value is much lower than that of an alkali metal. So Y is an alkaline earth metal."}
